#
# Per-asset facts about THIS building, plus the triples that join the two LBNL
# systems into one connected graph.
#
# Section 1 is the join. LBNL publishes the air handler and the chiller plant as
# two separate files with no statement connecting them and, inside the chiller
# plant file, no flow direction at all -- 0 brick:feeds triples in 191. Every
# edge in section 1 is authored here. Without them, upstream traversal from the
# air handler's cooling coil returns nothing and cross-asset root cause analysis
# is impossible.
#
# Sections 2 and 3 are the mvn: values the vocabulary in extensions.ttl declares.
#

@prefix mvn:     <https://aqueduct-pdm.local/mvn#> .
@prefix site:    <https://aqueduct-pdm.local/site#> .
@prefix brick:   <https://brickschema.org/schema/Brick#> .
@prefix sdahu:   <https://aqueduct-pdm.local/sdahu#> .
@prefix chiller: <https://aqueduct-pdm.local/chiller#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE WATER-SIDE FLOW PATH  (all authored -- none of this is from LBNL)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Direction convention: A brick:feeds B means what A does affects B. Traversal
# in the opposite direction -- from a symptom to its possible causes -- is the
# inverse, brick:isFedBy, written ^brick:feeds in the queries.
#
# The full authored chain, and the whole point of this file:
#
#   Cooling_Tower_n -> CDW_Loop -> Chiller_n -> CHW_Loop -> Cooling_Coil
#
# so a fouled cooling tower is upstream of the air handler's coil, four hops
# away, across two systems that LBNL never connected. The chain is deliberately
# acyclic. A real water loop is a closed circuit, and modelling it as one would
# make every asset upstream of every other and root cause analysis meaningless,
# so each loop is modelled in the single direction along which a fault
# propagates.
#

site:CHW_Loop a brick:Chilled_Water_Loop ;
    rdfs:label "Building chilled water loop" ;
    rdfs:comment """The chilled water circuit between the chiller plant and the
air handler's cooling coil. Exists as a node rather than a direct chiller-to-coil
edge because three chillers and five pumps all feed one coil: a node makes that
a five-plus-one edge fan-in instead of fifteen edges, and it is the thing that is
actually shared.""" .

chiller:Chiller_1 brick:feeds site:CHW_Loop .
chiller:Chiller_2 brick:feeds site:CHW_Loop .
chiller:Chiller_3 brick:feeds site:CHW_Loop .

chiller:Primary_Chilled_Water_Loop_Pump_1 brick:feeds site:CHW_Loop .
chiller:Primary_Chilled_Water_Loop_Pump_2 brick:feeds site:CHW_Loop .
chiller:Primary_Chilled_Water_Loop_Pump_3 brick:feeds site:CHW_Loop .
chiller:Secondary_Chilled_Water_Loop_Pump_1 brick:feeds site:CHW_Loop .
chiller:Secondary_Chilled_Water_Loop_Pump_2 brick:feeds site:CHW_Loop .

# THE EDGE. This single triple is what makes the two systems one graph.
site:CHW_Loop brick:feeds sdahu:Cooling_Coil .

chiller:Chilled_Water_System brick:hasPart site:CHW_Loop .

site:CDW_Loop a brick:Water_Loop ;
    rdfs:label "Condenser water loop" ;
    rdfs:comment """The condenser water circuit between the cooling towers and
the chillers. Modelled tower-to-chiller because that is the direction faults
travel: a fouled or fan-failed tower returns warmer water to the chiller, the
chiller has to work against a higher condensing temperature, and its capacity and
efficiency fall. This is the path the condenser fouling scenario depends on.

Typed brick:Water_Loop, not brick:Condenser_Water_Loop, because Brick 1.3 has no
such class -- it defines Loop, Water_Loop, Chilled_Water_Loop, Hot_Water_Loop,
Domestic_Water_Loop and Air_Loop, and the condenser case is simply missing.
Checked against the published ontology rather than assumed. Water_Loop is the
most specific class that actually exists; the condenser-water meaning is carried
by the label and by what this node connects to.""" .

chiller:Cooling_Tower_1 brick:feeds site:CDW_Loop .
chiller:Cooling_Tower_2 brick:feeds site:CDW_Loop .
chiller:Cooling_Tower_3 brick:feeds site:CDW_Loop .
chiller:Condenser_Water_Pump_1 brick:feeds site:CDW_Loop .
chiller:Condenser_Water_Pump_2 brick:feeds site:CDW_Loop .
chiller:Condenser_Water_Pump_3 brick:feeds site:CDW_Loop .
chiller:Threeway_Valve brick:feeds site:CDW_Loop .

site:CDW_Loop brick:feeds chiller:Chiller_1 .
site:CDW_Loop brick:feeds chiller:Chiller_2 .
site:CDW_Loop brick:feeds chiller:Chiller_3 .

chiller:Condenser_Water_System brick:hasPart site:CDW_Loop .

#
# THE AIR-SIDE CONTINUATION. Also authored, and added in checkpoint 6.3 to close a
# gap that only showed up once the API served a downstream trace.
#
# LBNL publishes exactly one flow statement on the air side -- sdahu:AHU feeds each
# of the five zones -- and section 1 above lands the chilled water on
# sdahu:Cooling_Coil. Those two facts do not join up. The coil is a brick:hasPart of
# the air handler, which is CONTAINMENT and not flow, so a traversal following
# brick:feeds from a chiller arrives at the coil and stops there. Asked who a failing
# chiller affects, the platform could answer "the air handler" and could not answer
# "two hundred people", which is the answer that decides whether the work gets
# scheduled tonight or next quarter.
#
# The missing statements are the air path itself: the coil conditions air, the supply
# fan moves it, the zones receive it. With them the chain runs
#
#   Cooling_Tower -> CDW_Loop -> Chiller -> CHW_Loop -> Cooling_Coil
#                            -> Supply_Air_Fan -> Zone_1..5
#
# so occupant impact is reachable from any water-side fault, six hops out at the
# furthest.
#
# Whether the fan sits before or after the coil in this unit is not determinable from
# the published data and does not matter here: either order gives the same
# reachability, and reachability is all these edges are used for. No hop count in
# app.asset_edges changes, because every node named below already maps to ahu-1 and
# the edge cache drops self-edges.
#
sdahu:Cooling_Coil brick:feeds sdahu:Supply_Air_Fan .

sdahu:Supply_Air_Fan brick:feeds sdahu:Zone_1 .
sdahu:Supply_Air_Fan brick:feeds sdahu:Zone_2 .
sdahu:Supply_Air_Fan brick:feeds sdahu:Zone_3 .
sdahu:Supply_Air_Fan brick:feeds sdahu:Zone_4 .
sdahu:Supply_Air_Fan brick:feeds sdahu:Zone_5 .

#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. ASSET ATTRIBUTES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Capacities below are measured from the fault-free run, not assumed. Each
# chiller's peak evaporator load over the year is 476 kW, which is 135 tons of
# refrigeration (1 ton = 3.517 kW, the rate that melts a ton of ice in a day and
# the unit chillers are sold in). Plant peak is 903 kW, 257 tons.
#
# ALL COST FIGURES ARE ESTIMATES AND NEED REPLACING WITH REAL QUOTES. They are
# derived from installed cost per ton at 2024 US commercial rates: about
# $1,200/ton for a water-cooled chiller, $400/ton for a cooling tower. They are
# the right order of magnitude and no better than that. Every one is a single
# edit here, and nothing computes them.
#

site:Building
    mvn:electricityTariffUSDPerKWh 0.128 ;
    mvn:labourRateUSDPerHour 95.00 ;
    rdfs:comment """Site-level economics. Both are estimates and both need
replacing with the actual numbers from a utility bill and a maintenance contract.

0.128 USD per kWh is the 2024 US commercial average retail rate. It is a flat
energy rate with no demand charge, which understates the cost of any fault that
raises the monthly peak -- a chiller losing efficiency on the hottest afternoon of
the year is exactly such a fault, and the real bill would be worse than this
project reports.

95 USD per hour is a fully loaded contractor rate for a commercial HVAC
technician, meaning wages plus overhead plus vehicle, not a wage.

Asserted on a site node rather than on any asset because a building buys
electricity and labour once, and because every advisory has to price its work
against the same rate or the priority ranking is comparing incomparable
numbers.""" .

sdahu:AHU
    mvn:criticalityTier 1 ;
    mvn:replacementCostUSD 95000.00 ;
    mvn:repairCostUSD 9000.00 ;
    mvn:occupantsServed 200 ;
    rdfs:comment """Tier 1: it is the only air handler in the modelled scope, so
if it stops, all five zones lose conditioned air immediately with nothing to pick
up the load. Occupants estimated at 40 per zone across the five zones it serves,
and asserted per zone below rather than only in total, so that a fault traced to
one zone can report the people in that zone rather than the whole building.""" .

#
# Occupants per zone. The five zones are the only nodes in this model that are
# downstream of the air handler, so they are what a "who does this affect" trace
# terminates on, and until now they carried no occupancy at all -- the trace could
# name the zones but not the people in them.
#
# Forty each, which is the per-zone figure the air handler's comment above already
# assumed, now written where a traversal can read it. The five sum to the 200 on
# the air handler, so the asset-level number and the zone-level numbers are
# consistent by construction and either can be checked against the other.
#
sdahu:Zone_1 mvn:occupantsServed 40 .
sdahu:Zone_2 mvn:occupantsServed 40 .
sdahu:Zone_3 mvn:occupantsServed 40 .
sdahu:Zone_4 mvn:occupantsServed 40 .
sdahu:Zone_5 mvn:occupantsServed 40 ;
    rdfs:comment """Estimated, not measured. The LBNL air handler dataset publishes
a zone temperature per zone and an occupancy schedule for the unit as a whole, but
no headcount and no floor area, so there is nothing in the source data to derive a
per-zone occupancy from. Forty per zone is a plausible density for an office floor
plate of this size and is uniform across the five zones because nothing in the data
distinguishes them. If real headcounts arrive, these five numbers are the only
edit; every occupant figure the advisory layer reports is summed from them.""" .

chiller:Simulated_Chiller_Plant
    mvn:criticalityTier 1 ;
    mvn:occupantsServed 200 ;
    rdfs:comment """Deliberately carries no replacement cost: a plant is not
purchased or replaced as a unit, its machines are, and putting a number here
would let the remaining-life layer double-count it against the chillers and
towers below. Occupants matches the air handler -- the modelled building is the
same one. Note the mismatch this exposes between the two LBNL datasets: 257 tons
of installed plant would normally serve a building many times larger than a
five-zone air handler. The two systems were simulated independently and were
never sized against each other.""" .

chiller:Chiller_1
    mvn:criticalityTier 1 ;
    mvn:replacementCostUSD 165000.00 ;
    mvn:repairCostUSD 18000.00 ;
    mvn:occupantsServed 200 .

chiller:Chiller_2
    mvn:criticalityTier 1 ;
    mvn:replacementCostUSD 165000.00 ;
    mvn:repairCostUSD 18000.00 ;
    mvn:occupantsServed 200 .

chiller:Chiller_3
    mvn:criticalityTier 1 ;
    mvn:replacementCostUSD 165000.00 ;
    mvn:repairCostUSD 18000.00 ;
    mvn:occupantsServed 200 ;
    rdfs:comment """All three chillers are tier 1 and carry the full occupant
count each, even though three machines against a 257-ton peak means losing one
does not by itself interrupt anyone. Whether redundancy should discount
criticality is an advisory-layer decision that needs the staging state at the
time of the fault, not a fact about the machine, so it is not baked in here.
Repair cost is a condenser tube clean or a refrigerant recharge, roughly 11% of
replacement.""" .

chiller:Cooling_Tower_1
    mvn:criticalityTier 2 ;
    mvn:replacementCostUSD 55000.00 ;
    mvn:repairCostUSD 6000.00 ;
    mvn:occupantsServed 200 .

chiller:Cooling_Tower_2
    mvn:criticalityTier 2 ;
    mvn:replacementCostUSD 55000.00 ;
    mvn:repairCostUSD 6000.00 ;
    mvn:occupantsServed 200 .

chiller:Cooling_Tower_3
    mvn:criticalityTier 2 ;
    mvn:replacementCostUSD 55000.00 ;
    mvn:repairCostUSD 6000.00 ;
    mvn:occupantsServed 200 ;
    rdfs:comment """Tier 2, not 1: a fouled tower makes the chillers work harder
and costs energy and capacity, but cooling continues. It degrades rather than
interrupts, which is the tier boundary. Fill replacement and a basin clean are
the usual repairs.""" .

#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. POINT DESIGN VALUES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every value here is read off the fault-free run rather than invented, so a
# reviewer can check any of them with one query. Units are SI, matching
# app.measurements. Where the source setpoint is constant all year, that constant
# IS the design value. Where it follows a reset schedule, the design value is the
# most demanding end of the schedule, because that is the condition the equipment
# was sized for.
#

sdahu:SA_TEMP
    mvn:designValue 12.88 ;
    rdfs:comment """degC. The supply air temperature setpoint is a hard constant
12.88 degC (55 degF) for all 105,036 samples of the fault-free year, so this is
the design value exactly and not an approximation of one.""" .

sdahu:SA_SP
    mvn:designValue 400.4 ;
    rdfs:comment """Pa. Duct static pressure setpoint, constant across the whole
fault-free year (1.607 inches of water). The design value itself is sound.

The claim that used to sit here -- that the measured pressure tracks the setpoint,
so the fan control loop works and the sensor is scaled consistently with it -- was
read off the fault-free file alone and is false for the other twenty. Those report
the setpoint as -400.25253 and the sensor in inches of water rather than Pascals,
so the two neither track nor share a scale. Both points are marked DO NOT USE in
ingestion/manifests/sdahu.yaml; nothing computes anything from them.""" .

chiller:CWL_PRI_SW_TEMP
    mvn:designValue 6.67 ;
    rdfs:comment """degC. Primary chilled water supply temperature. The setpoint
runs a reset schedule between 6.67 and 12.22 degC (44 to 54 degF); 6.67 is the
design end -- the coldest water the plant is asked for, at peak load.""" .

chiller:CHL_SW_TEMP_1 mvn:designValue 6.67 .
chiller:CHL_SW_TEMP_2 mvn:designValue 6.67 .
chiller:CHL_SW_TEMP_3 mvn:designValue 6.67 ;
    rdfs:comment """degC. Each chiller's own leaving chilled water temperature,
same design value as the loop it feeds.""" .

chiller:CT_SW_TEMP_1 mvn:designValue 29.44 .
chiller:CT_SW_TEMP_2 mvn:designValue 29.44 .
chiller:CT_SW_TEMP_3 mvn:designValue 29.44 ;
    rdfs:comment """degC. Condenser water temperature leaving the tower. The
tower setpoint resets between 15.56 and 29.44 degC (60 to 85 degF); 29.44 is the
design end here, which is the reverse of the chilled water case -- for a tower
the demanding condition is the hottest, most humid day, when it can only just
reach 85 degF. This is the reference the tower approach calculation needs (the
approach being the gap between the water the tower produces and the wet bulb
temperature, which is the coldest it could theoretically reach; a tower that
fouls loses the ability to close that gap).""" .

chiller:CWL_SEC_DP
    mvn:designValue 239364.7 ;
    rdfs:comment """Pa. Secondary loop differential pressure setpoint, constant
all year. FLAGGED: 239 kPa is about 35 psi, which is high for a secondary chilled
water loop where 10 to 20 psi is typical. The measured value tracks the setpoint
closely (238-242 kPa), so the sensor and control loop are at least self
consistent, but the native unit declared in the ingestion manifest (inches of
water) has not been independently confirmed against the LBNL documentation.""" .

chiller:CWL_SEC_LOAD
    mvn:designValue 903127.0 ;
    rdfs:comment """W. Design cooling load, taken as the annual peak of the
secondary loop load in the fault-free run -- 903 kW, 257 tons. Needed as the
denominator of part-load ratio (how hard the plant is working as a fraction of
what it can do), which the baseline layer needs because a chiller's efficiency
depends far more on part-load ratio than on anything else.""" .

chiller:CHL_POW_1 mvn:designValue 229398.0 .
chiller:CHL_POW_2 mvn:designValue 229398.0 .
chiller:CHL_POW_3 mvn:designValue 229398.0 ;
    rdfs:comment """W. Full-load compressor power, taken as the annual peak drawn
in the fault-free run. This is an observed peak standing in for a nameplate
rating, which is weaker than the setpoint-derived values above -- if the chiller
never reached full load in the simulated year, it is an underestimate.""" .

#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. SOURCE MISLABELLING, AND WHAT WAS DONE ABOUT IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two groups of readings were labelled wrongly in the source. Both are now
# corrected at the data layer rather than compensated for in each expression that
# touches them, because a compensation only works while everyone remembers it is
# there. Three places had to change together and all three now agree:
#
#   the graph      -- classes corrected in model/loader.py, NODE_CLASS_REPAIRS
#   the manifests  -- source column to point_id mapping, ingestion/manifests/
#   the database   -- scripts/fix_point_labels.sql, 10,090,368 rows relabelled
#
# The comments below stay on the nodes, because the LBNL source column names
# themselves cannot be changed -- they are the identifiers the CSVs use, and they
# remain misleading. Anyone reading the raw data needs this; anyone reading a
# point id or a class no longer does.
#

chiller:CWL_SEC_SW_TEMP
    rdfs:comment """SOURCE NAME IS WRONG, CORRECTED DOWNSTREAM. Named as a supply
water temperature, but carries the WARM return side: mean 11.97 degC over July
against 7.14 degC for CWL_SEC_RW_TEMP. Proof it is genuinely reversed rather than
an unfamiliar convention: the primary loop is labelled correctly at 7.19 degC
supply and 9.54 degC return, and a secondary loop is fed from the primary supply,
so secondary supply has to be about 7.19 -- which is what CWL_SEC_RW_TEMP holds.
Now typed brick:Chilled_Water_Return_Temperature_Sensor and mapped to point id
chw-plant-1.sec_return_temp.""" .

chiller:CWL_SEC_RW_TEMP
    rdfs:comment """SOURCE NAME IS WRONG, CORRECTED DOWNSTREAM -- see
CWL_SEC_SW_TEMP. Carries the cold water supplied to the loads. Now typed
brick:Chilled_Water_Supply_Temperature_Sensor and mapped to point id
chw-plant-1.sec_supply_temp, which is the node CoilEnergyBalance draws the coil's
entering water temperature from.""" .

chiller:CHL_SWCD_TEMP_1
    rdfs:comment """SOURCE NAME IS AMBIGUOUS, NOT REVERSED. Carries the warm water
leaving the condenser, 29.84 degC against 27.44 degC for CHL_RWCD_TEMP_1. That is
correct from the chiller's own point of view -- it supplies that water to the
tower -- but it is the opposite of the plant-level pair CDWL_SW_TEMP and
CDWL_RW_TEMP describing the same loop, where supply is the cool water the towers
send to the chillers (27.28 and 29.76 degC). One word, two senses, in one model.
Resolved by moving the chiller-level pair to Brick's entering and leaving classes,
which are defined explicitly and cannot be read two ways, and renaming the point
ids to chiller-n.cdw_leaving_temp and chiller-n.cdw_entering_temp.""" .
